TITLE:: SampleEdit
summary:: one sound file, read into the language and opened for editing
categories:: Libraries>SampleEditor
related:: Classes/SampleEditor, Classes/SampleAnalysis, Classes/SampleEditorHost

DESCRIPTION::
The model behind link::Classes/SampleEditor::, usable on its own: the file
is read into one link::Classes/Signal:: per channel, and every edit is a
setting on top of it - the trim, a gain, the DC taken out, fades on the edges
- until link::#-save:: writes the result over the file or link::#-saveAs::
writes it somewhere else. Nothing here needs the server. Every file written is
told to the edit's host (link::Classes/SampleEditorHost::), which may read it
back into a sample library.

code::
e = SampleEdit("~/x.wav");              // with SampleEditor.defaultHost
e = SampleEdit(path, SampleEditorDirtHost(~dirt));   // e.label is "bd:3" when ~dirt holds it
e.setRegion(0.1, 0.9); e.gainDb = -3; e.removeDC = true;
e.metrics;                              // (peak:, rms:, crest:, lufs: ...) of the result
e.metrics(false);                       // of the file as it is
e.save;                                 // over the file, .bak kept
e.revert;                               // the .bak back
e.addToBank("~/samples/kicks");         // the trim as kicks/kicks_00.wav, handed to the host
::

It also holds the beat grid - a bpm, where a beat falls, beats to the bar -
and, once link::#-analyseFeatures:: has run, what link::Classes/SampleFeatures::
says about the whole file: the onsets the slicer cuts on, the tempo, the key.

code::
e.analyseFeatures;                      // a few hundred ms on a 30 s loop
e.bpm; e.beatOffset;                    // the analysis's, until set
e.gridLines(1/16, 0, 2);                // [[seconds, beat] ...]
e.transients(0.7);                      // attack times at a sensitivity
e.refineOnset(e.firstTransient);        // the frame, to the sample
::

It sends code::changed:: with code::\loaded::, code::\params::, code::\saved::,
code::\savedAs::, code::\reverted::, code::\grid:: and code::\features::; the
editor window depends on it.


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
The file, read; named as the host's library knows it (link::#-name::,
link::#-index::) when it does.
ARGUMENT:: path
the file.
ARGUMENT:: host
nil for link::Classes/SampleEditor#*defaultHost::.

METHOD:: nextBankFile
The path link::#-addToBank:: would write: code::<folder>_<nn>.<ext>::, nn the
number of sound files in the folder or the first free number after it.
ARGUMENT:: folder
the bank's folder.
ARGUMENT:: ext
the extension.

METHOD:: soundFilesIn
The sound files right inside a folder (not its subfolders), sorted.
ARGUMENT:: folder
the folder.

METHOD:: samePath
Whether two spellings are one path: separators, a trailing slash, case on Windows.
ARGUMENT:: a
a path.
ARGUMENT:: b
another.

METHOD:: bankName
The bank SuperDirt makes of a folder: its name, with what Tidal's mini notation
would read as something else (spaces above all) as code::_::.
ARGUMENT:: folderPath
the folder.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Symbol::.

METHOD:: folderEntries
What is in a folder, sorted (glob characters in the name escaped).
ARGUMENT:: folder
the folder.

METHOD:: maxSamples
Frames times channels the editor will read; 24 million by default (about 4.5
minutes of stereo at 44.1 kHz). Reading takes about 0.2 s per million samples,
and sclang does nothing else meanwhile, so SuperDirt plays nothing while a long
file opens. Above link::#*hardLimit:: the hard limit holds.

METHOD:: hardLimit
536 million: the most the editor ever reads, whatever link::#*maxSamples:: is.
The whole file goes into one Signal, and sclang crashes, with no error to catch,
on any object of 2 GiB or more (536,870,912 floats) - about 101 minutes of
stereo at 44.1 kHz.

METHOD:: ceilingDb
What normalize brings the peak to; -1 dBFS by default.

METHOD:: backupSuffix
code::".bak"::, beside the file. Not a sound file extension, so the library
skips it.


INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: start
METHOD:: end
METHOD:: setRegion
The trim, as fractions of the file. The fades are clamped to fit inside it.

METHOD:: gainDb
METHOD:: removeDC
METHOD:: fadeIn
METHOD:: fadeOut
The gain in dB, whether the mean of each channel is taken out, and the sine
fades in seconds on the edges of the trim.

METHOD:: normalize
Set the gain so the loudest sample of the trim sits at the ceiling.

METHOD:: reset
The whole file again, no gain, no fades, the DC left in.

METHOD:: isPristine
Whether there is anything to save.

METHOD:: metrics
The readings of the result, or with code::false:: of the file as it is: the
keys of link::Classes/SampleAnalysis#*metrics:: plus code::lufs::,
code::momentary::, code::wholeBlock::, code::frames:: and code::duration::.

METHOD:: dcOffsets
The mean of each channel over the whole file.

METHOD:: peaks
METHOD:: samples
What the waveform view draws: min and max per column over a window of the
file, or the samples themselves for a window narrow enough.

METHOD:: render
The result as one interleaved Signal, clipped to a full-scale value when one
is given.

METHOD:: write
Write the result to a path in the file's own format, or a given one.

METHOD:: save
Over the file, keeping a code::.bak:: beside it the first time; then the
library's Buffer is re-read and this edit starts again from the saved file.
RETURNS:: the path, or nil when it could not.

METHOD:: renderRange
Any stretch of the file with the gain and the DC as set and fades of its own -
what a slice is written from.
ARGUMENT:: f0
the first frame.
ARGUMENT:: f1
the frame after the last.
ARGUMENT:: fadeInSecs
a sine fade in, seconds.
ARGUMENT:: fadeOutSecs
a sine fade out, seconds.
ARGUMENT:: clipTo
nil, or the amplitude to clip at.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Signal::, interleaved.

METHOD:: writeRange
The same to a file, in the file's own format unless told otherwise.
ARGUMENT:: outPath
ARGUMENT:: f0
ARGUMENT:: f1
ARGUMENT:: fadeInSecs
ARGUMENT:: fadeOutSecs
ARGUMENT:: header
ARGUMENT:: format
As for link::#-renderRange:: and link::#-write::.
RETURNS:: the path, or nil.

METHOD:: analyseFeatures
link::Classes/SampleFeatures#*analyseChannels:: on the whole file. Inside a
Routine with code::SampleFeatures.breathing:: set it lets the rest of sclang
in. The grid takes the analysis's bpm when it has none. A save or a revert
reads the file again and drops the features.
RETURNS:: the features, an Event.

METHOD:: features
What link::#-analyseFeatures:: found, or nil.

METHOD:: bpm
The grid's bpm, 20 .. 999; nil, no grid.

METHOD:: beatOffset
Where a beat of the grid falls, seconds; the grid runs both ways from it.

METHOD:: beatsPerBar
Four by default: a bar line every so many beats.

METHOD:: beatSeconds
60 / bpm, or nil.

METHOD:: gridLines
The grid's lines between two times.
ARGUMENT:: division
of a whole note: 1/4 a beat, 1/16 a sixteenth.
ARGUMENT:: from
seconds.
ARGUMENT:: to
seconds; nil for the end.
RETURNS:: code::[[seconds, beat] ...]::, the beat fractional between beats.

METHOD:: transients
ARGUMENT:: sensitivity
0 .. 1, see link::Classes/SampleFeatures#*pickOnsets::.
RETURNS:: the attack times, seconds; empty before link::#-analyseFeatures::.

METHOD:: firstTransient
Where the first hit is, seconds: the first attack at a high sensitivity, or
where the sound starts if that is earlier.

METHOD:: refineOnset
An attack time made exact: the first sample within 15 ms either side that
reaches 30% of the loudest there, a millisecond earlier, back to where channel
0 crosses zero.
ARGUMENT:: seconds
a time from the analysis.
RETURNS:: a frame.

METHOD:: saveAs
To another path; into the bank's folder and the host appends it to the bank
(link::Classes/SampleEditorHost#-fileAdded::).
RETURNS:: the path, or nil.

METHOD:: addToBank
The trim as a new file in a bank's folder, then handed to the host
(link::Classes/SampleEditorHost#-folderWritten::), which loads it when it knows
a library. A folder that exists gets one more
file, a new one is made. The file is named after the folder with the next free
number (link::#*nextBankFile::), in link::#-bankFormat::, with the gain, the DC
and the fades as set, and 1 ms in / 4 ms out where it cuts into the file. With
a bpm the file is given it in the host's tag store, when there is one, and queued for analysis.
ARGUMENT:: folder
the bank's folder.
RETURNS:: code::(path:, bank:, index:)::, or nil.

METHOD:: bankFormat
code::[extension, header, sample format]:: of a file written for a bank: the
file's own, or WAV when the host's library would not read the file's kind
(link::Classes/SampleEditorHost#-soundExtensions::; SuperDirt reads no flac, ogg,
mp3). The slicer's export uses it too.

METHOD:: revert
The code::.bak:: back over the file.

METHOD:: hasBackup
METHOD:: backupPath
Whether and where the original is kept.

METHOD:: host
METHOD:: name
METHOD:: index
METHOD:: path
METHOD:: label
METHOD:: inLibrary
Where it came from.

METHOD:: sampleRate
METHOD:: numChannels
METHOD:: numFrames
METHOD:: energyBlocks
The mean square of the file in every 2 ms block (link::#-blockFrames::), the
channels summed, as read: no gain, the DC left in. Worked out the first time
it is asked for and kept until the file is read again.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/FloatArray::.

METHOD:: blockFrames
Frames in one energy block.

METHOD:: levelOf
The level of a stretch of the file: its loudest 10 ms, from the blocks wholly
inside it; a stretch under 10 ms is read whole. The slicer's keep judges slices by it.
ARGUMENT:: f0
the first frame.
ARGUMENT:: f1
the frame after the last.
RETURNS:: dB (an rms level), or code::-inf:: for silence.

METHOD:: duration
METHOD:: headerFormat
METHOD:: sampleFormat
METHOD:: channels
The file as read.
